  "text": "to a handful of days per year to avoid customer fatigue. But the goal of this modeling is to create a dataset that can be analyzed to determine the maximum technical potential load profile impact at any given point in the year, so the demand flexibility is dispatched nearly every day in the modeled dataset. Although the profile on any given day is realistic, the full annual load profiles do not reflect the realistic dispatch of HVAC load flexibility. Details on the appropriate use of the load profiles can be found in this document. To keep the logic simple and to generate the most comprehensive result that can support different future analysis, this measure applies to all dwelling units with either a heating or a cooling system (approximately 99.7% of the dwelling units modeled in ResStock); however, the heating load analysis in this document is done on only buildings with electric heating. With a 4°F offset during the 4-hour peak periods across all 51 states, this resulted in an average reduction of 57% in peak period heating electricity consumption during the winter months and a 54% reduction in peak period cooling electricity during the summer months. v This report is available at no cost from the National Laboratory of the Rockies (NLR) at www.nlr.gov/publications. Table of Contents Executive Summary .................................................................................................................................... v ResStock 2025 Release 1 Measure Documentation: HVAC Load Flexibility ........................................ 1 Accessing Results ................................................................................................................................... 1 Measure Summary .................................................................................................................................. 1 1 Technology Summary ..............................................................................................